<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portuguese Inquisition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1536">1536</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Inquisition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Portuguese Inquisition was a church tribunal established in Portugal in 1536 under pressure from the Spanish crown. It acted with particular severity against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a>, converts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> suspected of secret practice. It exercised strong cultural and intellectual authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>, and its name is also used in comparison with the literary inquisition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition.jpg" alt="Portuguese Inquisition" width="144" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eiiaeo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selo_utilizado_pela_Inquisi%C3%A7%C3%A3o_Portuguesa.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the late 15th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of Portugal lived in self-governing communities called judiarias, and the free practice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was guaranteed by law. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> allowed the Inquisition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1478, many Jews moved to Portugal. On 5 December 1496 King Manuel I ordered all Jews to leave or convert, and in April 1497 Jewish children under 14 were taken from families who chose to leave. In October 1497 those who remained were forcibly baptised, becoming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a>.</p><p>After years of negotiation between kings and popes, the tribunal was established on 23 May 1536 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>'s bull Cum ad nihil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">magis</a>. It also imposed censorship of printed books, beginning with a ban on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> in languages other than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The Goa Inquisition extended it to Portuguese India, and it was abolished in 1821.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Six courts were set up between 1536 and 1541, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, Tomar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>, Lamego and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>; from 1548 they were concentrated in Lisbon and Évora, and only after the 1560s, with Coimbra restored and a court founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, did the structure settle. Inquisitors acted as both investigators and judges, helped by lay familiares, usually nobles, who could make arrests.</p><p>A visit began with an Edict of Grace, usually 30 days, during which people were urged to confess and denounce others. Anonymous denunciations and statements obtained under torture were accepted; trials were secret, the accused did not know the charges or the witnesses, and there was no appeal. Sentences were read at autos de fé, public ceremonies with Mass and procession, while executions by fire or garrote were carried out elsewhere by the secular authorities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Henry Charles Lea counted 1,175 people burned alive between 1540 and 1794 by the courts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a>, another 633 burned in effigy and 29,590 otherwise punished, figures that may slightly underestimate the reality. The Jesuit António Vieira, imprisoned by the tribunal from 1665 to 1667, later led a movement against it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p>The archives, among the best preserved judicial archives of early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, are held mostly in the Arquivo Nacional da Torre do Tombo, though most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> records were destroyed. Alexandre Herculano wrote a history of its establishment between 1854 and 1859. On 12 March 2000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> apologised on behalf of the Church for sins including those against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Portuguese Inquisition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Christians, converted Jews prominent as merchants, held civil and church offices, and the <b>Inquisition</b> was never permanently established.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Manuel I forced the conversion of the kingdom’s Jews in 1497, and the <b>Portuguese Inquisition</b>, founded in 1536, pursued these New Christians for two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Portuguese Inquisition</b> was established in 1536 at the request of King João III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic drew Portuguese and Spanish Jews fleeing the <b>Inquisitions</b> of Iberia in the 16th and 17th centuries, and later poorer German Jews.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>His defence of Copernicus brought his trial by the <b>Inquisition</b> in 1633.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-15">15 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Condemned by the <b>Inquisition</b> for heresy, the Dominican philosopher was burned in the Campo de' Fiori.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
